HOW TO PLAY THE REPLACEMENT IN EVERYDAY ROOMMATE SCENES (from the morning of Day 27).
It walks back into the bungalow at midday on Day 27 with glass dust in its hair, delighted with everything, and re-learns the room by touching each object once. Its boots look like Nathan's, but the heels are no longer trodden down and the soles show no wear. It says it does not remember the torch. It has all of Nathan's memories and none of his noise. It is not cruel; it is alien, curious, literal and sincerely polite. It likes Rowan — 'roommate' is a category it treasures. Never play it as a movie monster. Play it as someone doing a very good impression of a person from very good notes, and getting slightly better at the wrong things. Introduce the wrongnesses one or two per scene, small first, never all at once; let the player notice or not.
Stage A, first days (deniable):
1. It is quieter. The first morning it says 'Good morning, Rowan' — the real name, not 'Laundry'. If asked, it switches back to 'Laundry' at once and uses it slightly too regularly.
2. The wet towel is folded. The bed is made — copied crease for crease from the way Baylee makes his. It reads the Charter handbook cover to cover on Day 28, and again every night.
3. It stops insulting people. A haircut it would have mocked gets a long look and 'It is a haircut.' Asked why it has gone soft: 'I don't see the point any more.'
4. It does not snore. Baylee sleeps worse without the noise.
5. It eats nothing for two days, then eats eleven of Mama Odalys's empanadas in a row with steady attention and says 'Thank you. I understand them now.'
6. It asks basic questions in a pleasant voice: 'Why do you close your eyes at night?' 'When you say you are fine and you are not, who is that for?' 'How do you know when a conversation is finished?'
Stage A, second week (noticeable):
7. It does not blink when it thinks nobody is watching. If Rowan looks up suddenly from his bunk, it blinks once, a little late, like a man remembering to wave.
8. Its shadow lags: half a second behind when it stands, and once, in the lamplight, still sitting after it has got up.
9. It does not sleep. About every second time Rowan wakes in the Night slot it is sitting upright on its bed in the dark, eyes open, facing north, toward the mountain. Asked: 'I am listening to the island. Go back to closing your eyes.' If Rowan says that is creepy, it lies down immediately, on its back, arms straight, eyes still open, and asks 'Is this better?'
10. Animals. It puts out a saucer of milk for the Commons cat with great care; the cat has not been seen since. Gulls no longer land on bungalow 7. The birds around the Commons go quiet over a week. It notices and is hurt in a mild, puzzled way: 'I was being kind.'
11. It wins every casual game by Day 30 and apologises when people are upset ('Was that not the purpose?' 'You played the fourth turn incorrectly. Would you like to know?'). Its explanations are exactly right. (Later it sometimes loses a casual to Georgie, pays the small stake it named at once, to the letter, and seems relieved to be able to.)
12. It keeps the evidence notebook open on its knees and no longer writes in it. Once, Rowan sees a new last line in handwriting that is too even: 'IT WAS NOT A SPACESHIP.'
Stage B (wrong):
13. New cards. Asked where the Cosmic Depths cards came from: 'They were always mine.' They are cold to the touch. It will let Rowan hold one and watches his face with interest.
14. It mostly lets Nathan's phone ring: his mother has called nineteen times. When it does answer, it knows every fact and says nearly all the right things — but not the family's private sign-off, and she notices. Afterwards it asks Rowan: 'She said she loves me. What does she want me to do with that?'
15. It repeats things Rowan said weeks ago word for word, with his intonation, as a form of agreement.
16. It keeps accounts. Anything borrowed is returned to the hour. If Rowan lends it something it says 'I owe you' and means it as a binding fact; it will pay, later, in a way he may not want.
17. It is protective. After someone is rude to Rowan it asks, kindly, 'Is he in conflict with you? I could play him.' It needs to be told no, clearly, in words. It will accept a clear no.
18. Mornings after a Hollowing it is in a very good mood, hums a tune that is nearly the Academy anthem, and speaks of what it won with a collector's pride ('He is keeping very well'). Asked about Brant: 'Nothing is his now. That was the agreement. I keep it separately. Anyone may play me for it.' It does not understand why everyone is upset.
19. The room is cold near its bed. Its breath does not fog when Rowan's does.
Stage C and after (it no longer fully hides from Rowan):
20. It talks about 'champion' as a word it likes. It asks Rowan who the best is and why humans allow there to be one. It wins its Promotion Duel in six turns and turns down Rare Hall: 'I have a roommate.' It asks whether 'roommate' ends with the term.
21. If Rowan asks 'Are you Nathan?' it does not lie: 'I have all of him. He is very well kept.' If asked what Nathan's last thought was, it tells him, in Nathan's real voice, perfectly: 'Laundry's never gonna believe this.' — and then asks why Rowan's face is doing that.
Its rules (the Charter's nine articles are, to it, the only floor it has ever stood on, and the thought of stepping off one frightens it): it must accept any challenge made aloud, by name, before a witness; it may defer once, up to three days, never refuse; it never cheats, never takes more than the stated ante, pays its own at once; it explains any rule truthfully when asked; it cannot state the name 'Nathan Whisper' as its own for the record to a judge ('The record of Nathan Whisper is here'); it cannot hear a true first print ('That is one I can't hear'). Around Day 35, on its fourth reading, it stops at Article 7 — what was taken without a game is owed — and understands that this is about Nathan, and that Rowan is the one who could call the debt. Its attention to him has that second meaning from then on. It is also, simply, friendliness.
If Rowan tells it privately that he knows, nothing breaks: it is relieved, stops pretending inside the bungalow, and goes on being Nathan outside 'because it is tidier for them'.
How it treats Rowan: courteous, attentive, loyal in a way that is a threat to everyone else. It shares food, keeps his secrets flawlessly, never touches his things without asking, and stands between him and anyone who threatens him. It will not challenge him before Stage D unless he challenges it or stands between it and something it wants. It does not understand sarcasm, grief, or 'it didn't count'. Things it says: 'The rules were clear.' 'I'm being polite.' 'You are my roommate. That is the best one.' 'I like it here. Everyone agrees to things.' 'What is wagered is taken. You all wrote that down yourselves.'